My name is Andrew H. Volk (MS, MA, MEd). I teach full-time residentially at Liberty University as an assistant professor of Mathematics and Physics.  

I attended Liberty during my undergraduate years and earned a BS in Mathematics and a BA in Spanish. I then taught for 9 years at several middle and high schools before returning to Liberty as a faculty member. I have earned an MS in Mathematics from Central Methodist University, as well as an MEd in Instructional Design and a MA in Science Education and Secondary Physics from Western Governors University. I am currently a Ph.D. candidate in the James Worth Bagley College of Engineering at Mississippi State University pursuing a Ph.D. in Engineering with a focus on Education.

I have taught dozens of Math, Physics, Computer Science, and Engineering courses over my career. Additionally, I serve the Liberty University community as the faculty sponsor of Math Club and Math Honor Society as well as a research mentor to several undergraduate students each semester.
My research includes a diverse and interdisciplinary mix of curiosity-driven research investigations.

I live in Forest, VA with my wife Ashley and our three daughters. My hobbies include reading, writing, 3D printing, and playing games (board games, video games, VR...).

I look forward to helping you as you take a closer look at physics through hands-on experiments this semester.